  "query_preview": "/FRIDAY THROUGH WEDNESDAY/...\nIssued at 1205 AM MST Thu Nov 26 2026\n\nModels in much better agreement tonight through the extended\nperiod. A quick moving trough will bring a few mountain snow\nshowers to the Central Mountains Saturday, along with strong\nwinds for the Plains.\n\nFriday...increasing northwesterly flow aloft continues on Friday\nthrough the overnight hours. Enhanced mixing will lead to\nwarming conditions, with most areas on the Plains well into the\n60s, with a few areas near 70 degrees. The San Luis Valley will\nwarm into the 50s. Winds will also be on the increase,\nespecially in gap wind prone areas along the I-25 corridor.\nGusts 30 to 35 mph may be possible. Humidity values look to\nremain elevated above 20 percent. While dry conditions are\nforecast on Friday, a couple of light snow showers may move into\nthe Central Mountains late in the day. During the overnight\nhours, a shortwave trough is forecast to strength across Utah,\nforcing flow more westerly across southern Colorado. Showers\nlook to increase in coverage from the La Garita Range, north\ninto the Central Mountains. Little if any snow accumulations are\nforecast overnight. Winds will also remain elevated across the\nhigher terrain and along the lee slopes of the Eastern\nMountains. Overnight lows will fall into the 20s for the San\nLuis Valley, with the Plains ranging from the 40s west of I-25,\nto 30s east.\n\nSaturday and Sunday...the upper trough is forecast to track\nacross southern Colorado Saturday, clearing into the Central\nPlains by Sunday morning. Snow showers will be on the increase\nacross the higher terrain, with the main focus across the\nCentral Mountains. Snow accumulations look minor, with 1 to 3\ninches possible north of Cottonwood Pass through Saturday\nevening.  \n\nWinds will be the main concern across the Plains on Saturday. A\nvery strong cold front will drop south by mid to late morning,\nshifting winds northerly into the afternoon. Wind gusts 40 to 50\nmph will be possible, with higher gusts near the Kansas border.\nDESI 58 mph probs are 30-50 percent for portions of the Plains,\nand we will continue to monitor trends for possible high wind\nhighlights. Travel could be difficult on west-east highways due\nto strong cross winds.  \n\nAnother concern may be spotty rain and snow showers moving off\nthe higher terrain and into the Palmer Divide Saturday evening.\nTemperatures will be falling, from the 50s to lower 60s earlier\nin the day to 30s and 40s during the evening. Rain and snow\ndriven by strong winds may produce brief window for hazardous\ntravel Saturday evening north of Woodmen Road to Monument Hill.\nWe will continue to monitor this possibility.\n\nSnow winds down across the higher terrain overnight, ending by\nSunday morning. Across the Plains, a few showers may be possible\nbehind the front, mostly along the southern I-25 corridor.\nLittle if any impacts are expected outside of possibly the\nPalmer Divide. All precipitation should be done by Sunday\nmorning. Winds will remain brisk across the Plains on Sunday,\nwith highs on the Plains reaching the upper 30s to 40s.\n\nMonday through Thursday...broad zonal westerly flow is forecast\nto fill in behind the departing wave, with periods of light snow\nfor the Central Mountains, and warming conditions across the\nPlains, back into the 50s. The next storm system is possible\nWednesday into Thursday, but trends and differences in model\nguidance lead to uncertainty mid week.",
